1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,470
Jump out of a plane, and
how fast do you end up

2
00:00:03,470 --> 00:00:04,270
going?

3
00:00:04,570 --> 00:00:07,230
You'd think the higher you
start, the faster you'll

4
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:08,110
be by the end.

5
00:00:08,830 --> 00:00:10,090
But that's not how it works.

6
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:13,890
When you first jump, gravity
speeds you up quickly.

7
00:00:14,730 --> 00:00:17,650
But the faster you go,
the harder the air pushes

8
00:00:17,650 --> 00:00:18,450
back.

9
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:21,770
After about 12 seconds,
the push of the air matches

10
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:24,970
the pull of gravity, and
you stop speeding up.

11
00:00:25,790 --> 00:00:28,970
That top speed is called
terminal velocity.

12
00:00:29,790 --> 00:00:32,690
Lying flat on your belly
the way most skydivers

13
00:00:32,690 --> 00:00:35,730
fall, it's about 200
kilometres an hour.

14
00:00:36,450 --> 00:00:38,990
Turn head down so you're
narrow like a dart,

15
00:00:39,450 --> 00:00:44,690
and there's less air in
the way, about 240 to 290.

16
00:00:44,690 --> 00:00:49,470
Speed skydivers push
this as far as it goes.

17
00:00:49,470 --> 00:00:52,590
They jump from high up,
where the air is thinner,

18
00:00:53,130 --> 00:00:55,750
tuck in tight, and
point straight down.

19
00:00:56,450 --> 00:01:00,490
In August 2025, Spain's
Sebastián García Gutiérrez

20
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:05,370
set the official record,
an average of about 537

21
00:01:05,370 --> 00:01:08,170
kilometres an hour across
a whole competition.

22
00:01:08,970 --> 00:01:11,290
At the World Championships
this October, he went

23
00:01:11,290 --> 00:01:13,690
faster still, though
that's still waiting to be

24
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:14,490
made official.

25
00:01:15,030 --> 00:01:17,430
So once you've been falling
for 10 seconds or so,

26
00:01:17,770 --> 00:01:20,170
falling further doesn't
make you any faster.

27
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:23,130
The air sets your
speed, not the height.
